From 96318dcd6fe1131931db7fc3045be351337f3ee4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Til Goldan <til@goldan.dev>
Date: Wed, 8 Jul 2026 16:19:07 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] touchpad: don't treat a lone touch holding the click button
 as a palm

tp_palm_detect_pressure_triggered() classifies a touch as PALM_PRESSURE
whenever its reported pressure exceeds tp->palm.pressure_threshold, and
this check is deliberately unconditional/sticky (checked first and last
in tp_palm_detect(), "because it cannot be released and overrides all
other checks") -- unlike edge-based palm detection, it has no
multi-finger exemption at all.

On a capacitive/Force-Touch clickpad, physically pressing the button
measurably raises the sensed pressure of whichever touch is doing the
pressing, immediately crossing the palm pressure threshold on any real
click (not just a hard/deep one -- pressure crosses the threshold on
contact, unlike a contact-size-based check which would need actual
flattening). That touch gets marked PALM_PRESSURE and, being sticky,
stays excluded from tp_touch_active()/tp_touch_active_for_gesture() for
the rest of its lifetime -- so an ordinary 1fg click-and-drag never
moves the cursor: confirmed via debug-events showing "palm detected
(pressure)" logged right after BTN_LEFT pressed, for the sole touch
involved.

A touch that is the *only* one down and is actively holding the
clickpad button pressed cannot be a passively resting palm -- it's the
touch causing the click. Bypass (and clear, if already set) the
pressure-based palm classification for exactly that case. Restricted to
a single touch, same as the earlier thumb-jail fix: with two or more
fingers down, high pressure while clicking stays meaningful evidence of
a genuine resting palm.

Stacked on top of bazzite-til's 0001/0002/0003/0004 patches.
---
 src/evdev-mt-touchpad.c | 16 ++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/evdev-mt-touchpad.c b/src/evdev-mt-touchpad.c
index e60ced9b..ec0270e1 100644
--- a/src/evdev-mt-touchpad.c
+++ b/src/evdev-mt-touchpad.c
@@ -1037,6 +1037,22 @@ tp_palm_detect_pressure_triggered(struct tp_dispatch *tp,
 	if (!tp->palm.use_pressure)
 		return false;
 
+	/* A lone touch that is actively holding the clickpad button down
+	 * can't be a passively resting palm -- it's the touch causing the
+	 * click. Pressing a capacitive/Force-Touch clickpad measurably
+	 * raises the sensed pressure of whichever touch is doing the
+	 * pressing, which would otherwise get permanently misclassified as
+	 * a palm here (this check is normally sticky/unconditional, since a
+	 * real resting palm's pressure doesn't go away on its own). Only
+	 * bypass this for a single touch: with two or more fingers down,
+	 * treat pressure the same as before.
+	 */
+	if (tp->buttons.is_clickpad && tp->buttons.state && tp->nfingers_down <= 1) {
+		if (t->palm.state == PALM_PRESSURE)
+			t->palm.state = PALM_NONE;
+		return false;
+	}
+
 	if (t->palm.state != PALM_NONE && t->palm.state != PALM_PRESSURE)
 		return false;
 
